WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:04.833
- Oh, why she gotta ask
question like this, hold up!

00:00:06.448 --> 00:00:07.281
- Oh.

00:00:12.252 --> 00:00:13.303
- Oh, gosh.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:15.673
- Woof, okay.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.723
- I had a bad childhood, so that's a lot.

00:00:19.723 --> 00:00:22.706
(person laughs)

00:00:22.706 --> 00:00:25.623
(calm piano music)

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.481
- I kinda wish I never heard
what people think about me,

00:00:31.481 --> 00:00:34.718
because it really changes
my perspective on myself

00:00:34.718 --> 00:00:38.630
and it kinda drags my confidence down.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:41.933
A lotta people talk about
my weight or how I look,

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.800
and honestly, I feel like if
people kept their opinions

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.560
to themselves, that would
be a lot more helpful,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.573
but people are just opinionated I guess.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.420
- When I was five years
old, my mom told me I was

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:03.240
going to die, and I
remember being very shocked,

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.760
and I really got upset,
because it was the first time

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.563
that I realized I was going to die.

00:01:09.253 --> 00:01:13.340
- When my doctor told me I
had stage two lung cancer,

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:18.340
that would probably be
one of the big ones, yeah.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:27.770
This was in October of last year.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.972
I'm in college, so I went
to my local wellness center

00:01:30.972 --> 00:01:32.400
(person laughs)

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.090
when I started coughing
up blood, actually,

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.310
and then they referred me to a doctor

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:41.310
who then diagnosed me,
and I remember walking in,

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.020
and it was kind of your classic, like,

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.120
doctor on one side, you
sit on the other side

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.973
and he looks all somber, and he's like,

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.490
you have cancer, and then you're like, .

00:01:53.494 --> 00:01:56.293
(person laughing)

00:01:56.293 --> 00:02:00.550
And so, I kind of went into shock.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.630
I had taken public
transportation to the doctor

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:07.230
so I took public transportation back

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.343
and I was just like, in
my seat just zoning out,

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.950
and then I remember getting back,

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.940
calling my boyfriend at the time,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.833
telling him the news and just
sitting there in silence,

00:02:20.676 --> 00:02:25.676
and then after that call
ended I just burst into tears,

00:02:27.078 --> 00:02:28.918
and I was like, well, what do I do now?

00:02:28.918 --> 00:02:33.200
I had so many plans, and now I don't know

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.668
if I'll even be here to do them.

00:02:36.668 --> 00:02:39.740
I remember going back
into my dorm and having

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:44.115
to tell my suite mates and
having to comfort them.

00:02:44.115 --> 00:02:45.290
(person laughing)

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:47.540
And then going back into
my own room and just

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:52.733
trying to decide how to
reprioritize my life.

00:02:53.803 --> 00:02:56.110
I think that was the hardest part.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:00.306
My dad had non-small cell lung cancer,

00:03:00.306 --> 00:03:03.220
and I had small cell lung cancer.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.580
So we thought maybe it might be genetics,

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.490
but it wasn't connected.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:11.660
I don't smoke, I'm very young,
(person laughing)

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.263
so we still don't know.

00:03:14.441 --> 00:03:16.650
My family encouraged me
to drop out of school,

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:20.410
which I refused to do, 'cause
it's really important to me

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:25.410
that I graduate in four
years, so I stayed in school,

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:29.703
and then I think it was
four or five months later,

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:36.120
I beat cancer, technically,
finished my semester,

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.260
didn't fail any classes, which was good,

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:40.690
and I've been in remission ever since,

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:44.386
so hopefully I never
have to hear that again.

00:03:44.386 --> 00:03:47.800
That was around my birthday too, actually,

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:50.102
my 20th birthday, it
was like a week before.

00:03:50.102 --> 00:03:52.115
It's like, this is the
best birthday present

00:03:52.115 --> 00:03:54.320
I've ever gotten.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.650
Just being able to go back and tell people

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.660
that they didn't have to
worry about me any more,

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.865
at least for now, that was incredible.

00:04:01.865 --> 00:04:06.567
It was freedom, it was a lot of freedom,

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:10.840
to not be scared, to
pursue what I wanted to do.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:14.470
- My childhood best friend
passed away of cancer

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:19.470
when he was just 21 or 22,
and for a very long time

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:23.573
was very passionate about
being an airline pilot,

00:04:23.573 --> 00:04:26.610
and he was probably one of the most,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:30.290
was a kind of prodigy, so he was able

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.550
to go through flight
school really quickly,

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:36.553
and he was known as the baby
pilot because he was able

00:04:36.553 --> 00:04:39.140
to become an airline pilot so young

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.970
and he had such a baby face
that people could not believe

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:44.570
that he was already an airline pilot.

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.590
But then very soon after that,

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:49.150
he was not even diagnosed,
he just was walking,

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:53.601
I think, in Dubai or Abu
Dhabi on a flight trip,

00:04:53.601 --> 00:04:56.190
and he just collapsed on the floor,

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.200
and it turned out that he had like,

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.616
a cancer grew in his back
and it was just terminal.

00:05:01.616 --> 00:05:03.860
Actually has changed a little bit the

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:05.400
way I choose my career.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.440
Now I work in, the last
two company I've worked in

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.590
was all related with cancer,
and I realized that cancer

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:14.105
is a common thing that
maybe one of three people

00:05:14.105 --> 00:05:16.927
in your life will probably
suffer in their lifetime,

00:05:16.927 --> 00:05:20.174
and so I work on the
early cancer detection.

00:05:20.174 --> 00:05:21.780
There have been so many improvements

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:23.720
that within our generation,
I think it will be a disease

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:27.277
that I think we could eradicate
if we put the right people

00:05:27.277 --> 00:05:29.292
and the right money towards it.

00:05:29.292 --> 00:05:31.870
- [Interviewer] Can you
describe your best friend

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:33.552
to us the best you can?

00:05:33.552 --> 00:05:38.360
- Yeah, he was just like
a bubbly, happy guy,

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:43.360
just always in a good
mood and very positive.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:48.380
Maybe not the most extroverted,
but very passionate.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.340
I feel like I've found my
passion maybe later in life,

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.669
but he knew from the beginning
that he wanted to fly planes.

00:05:55.669 --> 00:05:58.930
I think it's always
amazing to see people have

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:02.930
such conviction in what they
want to do and pursue that

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:04.570
and make it happen and never give up

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:06.070
on their childhood dreams.

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:08.620
Your childhood dream is
like, you know, you listen

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:10.010
less to your brain or to your mind

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:11.268
but you listen to your heart,

00:06:11.268 --> 00:06:12.780
and often times you get older,

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:16.070
you listen more to your mind
and rationalize yourself

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:18.980
out of things that you
know is right for you,

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:21.795
but you just can't lose track of that,

00:06:21.795 --> 00:06:25.900
and I think he never lost
track of what his passion

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.350
of what he wanted as a kid and did it.

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.103
Although he didn't live very long,

00:06:30.103 --> 00:06:34.510
I think a life worth living is
not counted in years, right,

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.546
it's more about what
you do, what you become.

00:06:36.546 --> 00:06:38.530
It doesn't matter how short it is.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:42.481
I think his life was
worth every second of it,

00:06:42.481 --> 00:06:44.780
and made it count.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:46.560
- I wish I would have never heard

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.270
or at least read the suicide notes

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:53.130
of a boy in my class about
the way our school treated him

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.959
and why he did what he did.

00:06:54.959 --> 00:06:59.603
He just said that at least like
the neighborhood we lived in

00:06:59.603 --> 00:07:02.980
and the community we lived in
put too much pressure on kids

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:06.230
to go to the right school,
to go in the same path way,

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:07.910
to go to college after high school,

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.850
and how none of the teachers supported him

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:12.000
and none of the adults who had authority

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.600
would listen to him, but
it's really caused change

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:16.900
in my school, which is a positive result,

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:20.643
but I didn't really wanna know that kids

00:07:20.643 --> 00:07:22.650
were going through something like that.

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:23.550
It's kind of dark.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:28.803
- I wish I had never heard that
my friends sometimes use me.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:31.850
- Racism.

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:33.720
All my life I've heard about racism,

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:34.750
I've learned about it, I've studied it.

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:37.570
It was like, kinda unique topic to me,

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:39.330
'cause I never understood why

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:40.920
other people would look at
other people a certain way

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:42.140
and try to control them just because they

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:43.960
were a different color than they were,

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:45.070
but everyone's a person,

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:47.730
like you have an eyes,
nose, teeth, ears, a heart,

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:49.200
and everyone should be the same.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.820
I think that everyone's equal,

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.320
and you're not higher than anyone else.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:55.800
- One thing, 'cause we were
talking about it this morning,

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:59.326
is I wish I never heard
that the devil was real,

00:07:59.326 --> 00:08:04.326
or that, like, anything
about the devil as a child.

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:06.905
- I wish,

00:08:06.905 --> 00:08:07.738
(sucks teeth)

00:08:07.738 --> 00:08:12.323
I wish I'd never heard
about how meat was made,

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:14.755
or is made.

00:08:14.755 --> 00:08:15.800
(people laughing)

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:17.550
Because I like to eat meat, but I also

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:21.433
don't wanna add to animal
cruelty, but it's so good.

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:25.932
- I wished I never heard
that Santa was real,

00:08:25.932 --> 00:08:30.229
because since I was
little, I got two things.

00:08:30.229 --> 00:08:33.771
One thing, my house
doesn't have a chimney,

00:08:33.771 --> 00:08:34.604
(child giggles)

00:08:34.604 --> 00:08:39.604
and two things, how does one man know

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:40.696
when seven billion people are sleeping?

00:08:40.696 --> 00:08:43.251
(child giggles)

00:08:43.251 --> 00:08:47.083
Like, that makes no sense,
and I'm a very logical person.

00:08:48.427 --> 00:08:50.870
But I don't want my little
brother to be discouraged

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:55.472
or anything, so I'm just gonna
act like he's real, you know?

00:08:55.472 --> 00:08:57.824
(boy laughing)

00:08:57.824 --> 00:09:01.190
(calm piano music)

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:02.373
- Rude words.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:05.330
- [Interviewer] Do you have
any specific ones in mind?

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:06.890
- No.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:08.759
And if I had, I wouldn't say them.

00:09:08.759 --> 00:09:10.900
(person laughing)

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:13.123
- Uh, you might wanna get tested.

00:09:13.123 --> 00:09:14.885
(people laughing)

00:09:14.885 --> 00:09:18.893
- Probably that you're
not college material.

00:09:19.906 --> 00:09:20.739
Yeah.

00:09:20.739 --> 00:09:21.572
- [Interviewer] Do you
remember who told you that?

00:09:21.572 --> 00:09:22.600
- My grandmother.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.710
I, personally, think that
everybody's college material,

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:27.290
and it's just, coming from lower class,

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:30.776
it's hard to, obviously I'm
not going to go to Harvard.

00:09:30.776 --> 00:09:31.960
Who goes to Harvard?

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:33.350
Rich people, not me!

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:34.940
She tells the same thing to my sister.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:36.790
She tells me, like, your
sister's not college material,

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:38.390
and I'm like, you can't
say that to someone,

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:40.490
'cause it's gonna discourage
them from going to college.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:43.610
I'm already going to graduate
with my bachelor's debt-free,

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:46.390
and I've traveled the world,
and so anybody can do it.

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:47.887
It really discourages someone to say that,

00:09:47.887 --> 00:09:51.829
and I wanna go get my PhD, and
I wanna do all these things,

00:09:51.829 --> 00:09:54.170
but I don't know, it always kinda sticks

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:56.180
in the back of my mind, like,
you're not college material,

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:58.470
you're not gonna get
there, but like, I am.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.530
- I wish I'd never heard
the term fake news.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:04.414
Because I feel like the
people who work so hard,

00:10:04.414 --> 00:10:08.385
go to college, they try
to be so fair and balanced

00:10:08.385 --> 00:10:12.410
in their reporting, it's their life.

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.690
And ever since this
fake news rumor started,

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:18.846
nobody has a belief
anymore, in institutions,

00:10:18.846 --> 00:10:23.846
in the news media, in their leaders,

00:10:23.902 --> 00:10:27.220
and so the whole belief system is eroded

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:30.352
and that's what I wish I had never heard.

00:10:30.352 --> 00:10:33.053
- We don't have enough money.

00:10:33.053 --> 00:10:36.035
I'm a teacher and I'm from Arizona,

00:10:36.035 --> 00:10:39.920
and legislators are always
saying they don't have

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:42.510
enough money to fund our education system,

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:46.600
and as a result our children
are going to be illiterate.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:48.529
The teachers don't stay more than a year

00:10:48.529 --> 00:10:52.263
and they all go, and I,

00:10:52.263 --> 00:10:54.660
well, they have enough money.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.097
They're not using it to take care

00:10:56.097 --> 00:10:59.240
of what needs to be taken care of.

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:02.313
They put in the rainy day
fund, but it's already rainy.

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:09.460
- I wish I hadn't heard my dad, like,

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:12.412
talk about how much he cheated on my mom.

00:11:12.412 --> 00:11:15.340
I don't know, 'cause like
I'd saw him differently

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:16.849
every since then.

00:11:16.849 --> 00:11:19.120
- I wish I never had all this story

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:21.130
of so many religions in this world,

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:26.130
because I don't believe in religions.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:28.170
I believe there is one God,

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:31.270
but I don't believe in the religions.

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:35.970
Religions separate
brothers, sisters, friends,

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:40.380
create trouble to others, but
if you go through all of them

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:45.182
which have been in all of them,
everybody say God is good.

00:11:45.182 --> 00:11:47.163
So there's only one God.

00:11:48.695 --> 00:11:49.923
There is not religions.

00:11:51.238 --> 00:11:53.081
That's my own belief today.

00:11:53.081 --> 00:11:54.855
- My answer kind of answers the question,

00:11:54.855 --> 00:11:56.870
but it's a bit of a variety.

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:58.803
It's more of one thing
that I've heard repeatedly

00:11:58.803 --> 00:12:00.588
that I would like to have not heard,

00:12:00.588 --> 00:12:02.523
so hopefully that counts.

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:05.660
Something that I wouldn't
have liked to hear

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:10.660
is anytime my dad kind of yells at my mom

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:15.970
in a negative way or
if she makes a mistake

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:19.819
or if she slips up anywhere,
even though she's just tryin'a

00:12:19.819 --> 00:12:22.130
do the right thing, my dad
kinda gets angry at her.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:24.140
So I think anytime that's happened,

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:27.445
I would have enjoyed
not being part of that.

00:12:27.445 --> 00:12:29.520
I feel like a terrible bystander

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:31.500
just watching something bad happen,

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:33.800
like I love my mom, I love my dad.

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.470
My dad isn't a bad person by any means.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:38.087
He's actually a great
person, so is my mom.

00:12:38.087 --> 00:12:40.930
She just has good intentions,
and sometimes the execution

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:43.305
doesn't work out well,
which is just unfortunate

00:12:43.305 --> 00:12:47.004
and I think we all have
that so empathy where

00:12:47.004 --> 00:12:49.760
if you see someone trying really hard

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.190
but they don't quite
achieve whatever it is

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:53.840
that they're tryin' to do, that
you just feel bad for them,

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:57.230
because you tell everyone oh, if you try,

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.280
as long as you're trying,
everything's fine,

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:02.550
but there's obviously
incidents where you're trying

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:04.400
and things don't work
out, and that really sucks

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:07.115
and there's nothing you can
do about it, like, you know.

00:13:07.115 --> 00:13:10.914
- I think that's a hard
question for me specifically,

00:13:10.914 --> 00:13:14.059
because with the way my personality is,

00:13:14.059 --> 00:13:18.355
I would always rather
know, even if it hurts.

00:13:18.355 --> 00:13:21.584
Recently, maybe a month or two ago,

00:13:21.584 --> 00:13:24.005
I learned that my dad has cancer.

00:13:24.005 --> 00:13:26.670
And he didn't tell me.

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:30.001
I learned from an ex, actually,

00:13:30.001 --> 00:13:33.542
and my dad still hasn't told me about it,

00:13:33.542 --> 00:13:36.990
